{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017Zm6QokqPxvnsUrpNWExCg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LJ37fBMNoZL1Xtr8mQcjgu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus areas are **methods** and **experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Why Self-Consistency Fails for Structured Prediction: An Analysis of the Correlation–Selection Gap\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether consistency-based confidence signals can serve as quality estimators and sample selectors for structured information extraction (NER and RE). It compares 5 unsupervised confidence signals: log-probability, exact-match rate, Fleiss' κ, soft Jaccard (SJ), and entity voting confidence. Evaluated on 2 models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) × 2 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003), plus abandoned WNUT-17.\n\n**Main findings (negative):**\n- Structural consistency signals (SJ, voting) correlate only marginally more with F1 than log-probability on NER (Δρ = +0.01–0.04, ΔAUROC ≤ 0.04), not always statistically significant.\n- On RE (full set), log-probability matches or exceeds structural signals.\n- Best-of-N selection: ALL signals ≤ greedy on NER despite large oracle headroom (+15pp). On RE, only log-prob beats greedy (+2.3pp).\n- \"Correlation-selection gap\": instance-level consistency signals predict input difficulty, not per-sample quality. They assign the same score to all N samples from the same input → degenerate to random selection.\n- N=8→16 and signal ensembling don't close the gap.\n\n## Key Tables/Results\n\n**Table 1 (SciERC, N=8, T=1.0):**\n| Signal | NER ρ | NER AUROC | RE ρ | RE AUROC |\n|--------|-------|-----------|------|----------|\n| Voting | 0.37  | 0.68      | 0.30 | 0.64     |\n| SJ     | 0.36  | 0.67      | 0.21 | 0.59     |\n| Log-prob| 0.33 | 0.64      | 0.25 | 0.62     |\n| FK     | 0.27  | 0.59      | 0.25 | 0.61     |\n| EM     | 0.26  | 0.62      | 0.10 | 0.50     |\n| LOO-SJ | 0.35  | —         | —    | —        |\n\n**Table 3 (2×2 cross-validation, NER SJ ρ, SJ−LP Δρ):**\n| | Qwen3-8B | LLaMA-3.1-8B |\n|---|---|---|\n| SciERC | 0.36 (+0.03) | 0.36 (+0.06) |\n| CoNLL  | 0.34 (+0.02) | 0.34 (+0.03) |\n\n**Table 6 (Best-of-N selection F1):**\n| Signal | SciERC NER | SciERC RE | CoNLL NER |\n|--------|------------|-----------|-----------|\n| Greedy | 0.618      | 0.374     | 0.725     |\n| Log-prob| 0.609     | **0.397** | 0.716     |\n| SJ     | 0.604      | 0.368     | 0.718     |\n| Oracle | 0.769      | 0.561     | 0.757     |\n\n**Table 4 (N-scaling, SciERC):** NER SJ ρ@N=8=0.36, ρ@N=16=0.353 (Δ=−0.007). RE SJ ρ@N=8=0.21, ρ@N=16=0.28 (+0.07).\n\n**Table 5 (Temperature):** SJ at T=0.7 achieves ρ=0.39 (best), but log-prob is temperature-invariant (Δ=0.01).\n\n**Table 7 (Per-relation RE):** Log-prob exceeds SJ on 6/7 relation types. FEATURE-OF: SJ ρ≈0.\n\n**Table 8 (Stratification):** No clean inverted-U pattern. Log-prob exceeds SJ within every stratum.\n\n**Table 9 (OOD):** SJ drops 0.36→0.05 (Δ=−0.31), log-prob 0.33→0.15 (Δ=−0.18). \"Hallucination consistency\" failure mode.\n\n**Ensemble:** AUROC +0.02 over best single. Selection still ≤ greedy.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full experiment plan includes 17 experiments. The paper covers ALL of them:\n- exp-012 to exp-016: baseline comparisons, metrics expansion (Tables 1-2)\n- exp-001: N=16 scaling (Table 4)\n- exp-002: CoNLL-2003 (Table 2)\n- exp-003: WNUT-17 (reported despite abandonment, Table 2, Section 5.2)\n- exp-004: RE diagnostic (Table 7)\n- exp-005: Temperature (Table 5)\n- exp-006: Best-of-N (Table 6)\n- exp-007: LLaMA cross-model (Table 3)\n- exp-008: RE N=16 (Table 4)\n- exp-009: Capability stratification (Table 8)\n- exp-010: Selection curves (Section 5.6, text only)\n- exp-011: OOD robustness (Table 9)\n- exp-016: Signal ensemble (Section 5.6)\n- exp-017: LLaMA×CoNLL (Table 3)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (focus on methodology and experimental design)\n3. **Weaknesses** (focus on methodology and experimental design)\n4. **Questions for authors**\n5. **Minor issues**\n6. **Overall assessment** (1-2 sentences)\n7. **Scores**: Overall (1-6), Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe thorough, critical, and fair. Evaluate:\n- Is the experimental methodology sound?\n- Are the comparisons fair and comprehensive?\n- Are the statistical analyses adequate?\n- Is the evaluation protocol well-designed?\n- Are the conclusions supported by the evidence?\n- Are there methodological gaps or flaws?\n- Is the best-of-N selection protocol fair to instance-level signals?\n\nRemember: this is evaluated against top ML venue standards (~25% acceptance rate)."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":50736,"cache_read_input_tokens":22953,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":50736},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a188aefa-23b6-4688-b448-164a4ee4e586","uuid":"eed43daa-2573-430d-bbf1-453ad2e90bfe"}
