{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011iDHaXNox9b1tBgtxKCqHH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n4\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n8\t\\usepackage{pgfplots}\n9\t\\pgfplotsset{compat=1.17}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage[capitalise]{cleveref}\n12\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n13\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n14\t\n15\t\\definecolor{structblue}{RGB}{31,119,180}\n16\t\\definecolor{logprobred}{RGB}{214,39,40}\n17\t\\definecolor{surfacegray}{RGB}{127,127,127}\n18\t\\definecolor{oraclegreen}{RGB}{44,160,44}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Structural Self-Consistency for Quality Estimation\\\\in LLM-Based Information Extraction}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\\date{}\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{document}\n26\t\\maketitle\n27\t\n28\t% ============================================================\n29\t% ABSTRACT\n30\t% ============================================================\n31\t\\begin{abstract}\n32\tLarge language models increasingly tackle structured information extraction (IE) via multi-sample generation, yet practitioners lack principled guidance on which confidence signals to trust---and for what purpose.\n33\tWe systematically compare five unsupervised confidence signals spanning four families---model confidence (log-probability), surface agreement (Fleiss' $\\kappa$, exact match), set-aware structural similarity (Soft Jaccard), and entity-level majority voting---for named entity recognition (NER) and relation extraction (RE) across two models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B) and two datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003).\n34\tWe report three findings.\n35\t\\textbf{(1)}~Structural consistency signals provide the strongest unsupervised quality estimation: entity voting achieves AUROC 0.73 and $\\rho{=}0.50$ on NER, substantially outperforming log-probability (AUROC 0.56, $\\rho{=}0.14$).\n36\t\\textbf{(2)}~A \\emph{correlation--selection gap} exists: despite strong instance-level correlation, no unsupervised signal beats greedy decoding for NER best-of-$N$ selection; only log-probability enables selection on RE (+2.4pp).\n37\t\\textbf{(3)}~All signals degrade on relational tasks due to compound error propagation, but remain valid within the model's capability range.\n38\tThese results hold across a $2{\\times}2$ model--dataset matrix ($|\\Delta\\rho|{\\le}0.006$ cross-model) and three random seeds ($\\sigma_\\rho{\\le}0.008$).\n39\tOur findings disentangle quality estimation from sample selection and provide actionable guidance for deploying multi-sample IE systems.\n40\t\\end{abstract}\n41\t\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n44\t% ============================================================\n45\t\\section{Introduction}\n46\t\n47\tMulti-sample generation---drawing $N$ candidate outputs from a language model and selecting or aggregating among them---has become a standard tool for improving LLM performance on reasoning tasks~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency,snell2024scaling,brown2024tts}.\n48\tFor structured information extraction (IE), where outputs are entity sets or relation tuples, the natural question is: \\emph{which confidence signal should guide the selection or abstention decision?}\n49\t\n50\tThis question is more nuanced than it appears.\n51\tPrior work on self-consistency~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} uses exact-match voting, which conflates two distinct capabilities: (i)~\\emph{quality estimation}---predicting which inputs yield reliable outputs---and (ii)~\\emph{sample selection}---choosing the best output among $N$ candidates.\n52\tFor structured outputs, surface-level agreement metrics further suffer from semantic blindness: two entity sets may differ in surface form yet agree structurally~\\cite{nedellec2026consistency}.\n53\t\n54\tWe address these gaps with a systematic comparison of five confidence signals across four families for entity-level IE (\\cref{sec:method}).\n55\tOur study spans two tasks (NER, RE), two models (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B), two datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003), and evaluates both quality estimation (Spearman $\\rho$, Kendall $\\tau$, AUROC) and best-of-$N$ selection.\n56\t\n57\tOur contributions are:\n58\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n59\t\\item We show that structural consistency signals---entity voting confidence and Soft Jaccard---are the strongest unsupervised quality estimators for IE, outperforming log-probability by 17--30 AUROC points and $0.2$--$0.4$ in Spearman $\\rho$ (\\cref{sec:results-qe}).\n60\t\\item We identify a \\emph{correlation--selection gap}: strong instance-level quality prediction does not translate to effective per-sample selection. On NER, no signal surpasses greedy decoding despite 16pp of oracle headroom (\\cref{sec:results-gap}).\n61\t\\item We characterize a \\emph{task-complexity boundary}: compound error propagation in relational tasks degrades all consistency signals on the full instance set, but conditional filtering on the model's capability range restores discriminative power (\\cref{sec:results-boundary}).\n62\t\\item We validate these findings across a $2{\\times}2$ model--dataset matrix with cross-model $|\\Delta\\rho|{\\le}0.006$ and three-seed $\\sigma_\\rho{\\le}0.008$ (\\cref{sec:results-generalization}).\n63\t\\end{enumerate}\n64\t\n65\t% ============================================================\n66\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n67\t% ============================================================\n68\t\\section{Related Work}\n69\t\n70\t\\paragraph{Self-consistency for reasoning.}\n71\tWang et al.~\\cite{wang2023selfconsistency} introduced self-consistency as majority voting over chain-of-thought samples, demonstrating gains on arithmetic and commonsense reasoning.\n72\tSubsequent work has extended this to confidence-weighted voting (CISC;~\\citealt{cisc2025}), reliability-aware adaptive thresholds (ReASC;~\\citealt{reasc2026}), sub-claim decomposition (Atomic Self-Consistency;~\\citealt{atomic2024}), ranked voting~\\cite{rankedvoting2025}, and KL-divergence-based reward-free selection~\\cite{selfcertainty2026}.\n73\tAll of these target discrete-answer reasoning tasks; none addresses the set-structured outputs characteristic of IE.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty quantification for LLMs.}\n76\tKuhn et al.~\\cite{kuhn2023semantic} proposed semantic entropy over meaning-equivalent clusters.\n77\tSurveys by~\\citet{uqsurvey2025} and~\\citet{uqcalibsurvey2025} provide comprehensive taxonomies.\n78\tSemantic entropy probes~\\cite{semanticprobes2024} and latent self-consistency~\\cite{latentsc2025} reduce computational cost via embedding-space approximations.\n79\tManakul et al.~\\cite{manakul2023selfcheckgpt} use sampling-based self-checking for hallucination detection.\n80\tCoCoA~\\cite{cocoa2025} combines confidence and consistency via minimum Bayes risk; Vashurin et al.~\\cite{vashurin2025eval} systematically evaluate four UQ methods across tasks---none including structured IE.\n81\tOur work fills this gap by comparing confidence signal families specifically for entity-level and relational IE.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Calibration and selective prediction.}\n84\tModel calibration has been studied extensively~\\cite{guo2017calibration,kadavath2022language,desai2020calibration}.\n85\tSelective prediction---abstaining on uncertain inputs---is surveyed by~\\citet{geifman2017selective} and~\\citet{knowyourlimits2025}.\n86\tThe confidence estimation survey of~\\citet{confestsurvey2024} provides a broad taxonomy.\n87\tFor structured prediction, Kuleshov \\& Liang~\\cite{kuleshov2015structured} and Jagannatha \\& Yu~\\cite{jagannatha2022calibration} address calibration but not multi-sample consistency.\n88\tOur quality estimation results (\\cref{sec:results-qe}) directly apply to selective prediction / abstention.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Confidence in information extraction.}\n91\tCulotta \\& McCallum~\\cite{culotta2004confidence} studied confidence estimation for CRF-based IE.\n92\tFor NER, token-level uncertainty methods include MC-dropout~\\cite{gal2016dropout,xiao2019quantifying} and deep ensembles~\\cite{lakshminarayanan2017simple,shelmanov2021certain}.\n93\tN\\'{e}dellec \\& Xia~\\cite{nedellec2026consistency} recently studied consistency--accuracy correlation for hard-prompted LLMs on plant-health IE, but used surface-level string matching (which they acknowledge ``overlooks semantically consistent outputs''), a single domain, and did not study best-of-$N$ selection.\n94\tOur Soft Jaccard metric directly addresses their limitation, and our multi-signal comparison provides a more complete picture.\n95\t\n96\t\\paragraph{Test-time scaling and sample selection.}\n97\tSnell et al.~\\cite{snell2024scaling} and Brown et al.~\\cite{brown2024tts} formalize test-time compute scaling.\n98\tVerifier and reward models~\\cite{cobbe2021training,lightman2023lets} provide supervised selection signals.\n99\tOur work studies the unsupervised regime where such verifiers are unavailable, and identifies a fundamental gap between quality estimation and selection (\\cref{sec:results-gap}).\n100\tConformal prediction approaches~\\cite{fisch2021conformal,angelopoulos2021gentle} provide coverage guarantees but require held-out calibration data; our signals are fully unsupervised."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LJ37fBMNoZL1Xtr8mQcjgu","session_id":"a188aefa-23b6-4688-b448-164a4ee4e586","uuid":"53afebc6-2bf6-4586-a83f-665fc4bb0d58","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:31:56.598Z"}
