{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SQY5PpV7Ne1J487fsPyMe5","type":"tool_result","content":"100\tConformal prediction approaches~\\cite{fisch2021conformal,angelopoulos2021gentle} provide coverage guarantees but require held-out calibration data; our signals are fully unsupervised.\n101\t\n102\t% ============================================================\n103\t% 3. METHOD\n104\t% ============================================================\n105\t\\section{Method}\n106\t\\label{sec:method}\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n109\t\n110\tGiven an input $x$, a language model $\\mathcal{M}$ generates $N$ candidate structured outputs $\\{y_1, \\ldots, y_N\\}$ via temperature sampling ($T{>}0$).\n111\tEach output $y_i$ is a set of typed entities (NER) or relation tuples (RE).\n112\tA greedy reference $y_0$ is obtained via $T{=}0$.\n113\tWe study two problems: (i)~\\emph{quality estimation}---predicting instance-level output quality $q(x) = F_1(y_0, y^*)$ where $y^*$ is the gold standard, and (ii)~\\emph{sample selection}---choosing $\\hat{y} = \\arg\\max_{y_i} s(y_i)$ to maximize $F_1(\\hat{y}, y^*)$.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Confidence Signals}\n116\t\n117\tWe compare five signals spanning four families:\n118\t\n119\t\\paragraph{Log-probability (\\textsc{logprob}).}\n120\tThe mean token-level log-probability of the generated sequence:\n121\t$\\textsc{logprob}(y_i) = \\frac{1}{|y_i|} \\sum_{t=1}^{|y_i|} \\log p_\\theta(y_i^{(t)} \\mid x, y_i^{(<t)})$.\n122\tThis is the only \\emph{per-sample} signal: it scores each $y_i$ independently.\n123\tFor quality estimation, we use $\\textsc{logprob}(x) = \\textsc{logprob}(y_0)$, the greedy output's log-probability.\n124\t\n125\t\\paragraph{Exact Match (\\textsc{em}).}\n126\tThe fraction of sample pairs producing identical outputs:\n127\t$\\textsc{em}(x) = \\binom{N}{2}^{-1} \\sum_{i<j} \\mathbb{1}[y_i = y_j]$.\n128\t\n129\t\\paragraph{Fleiss' Kappa (\\textsc{fk}).}\n130\tToken-level inter-annotator agreement across $N$ samples, treating each sample as an annotator assigning BIO tags.\n131\t\n132\t\\paragraph{Soft Jaccard (\\textsc{sj}).}\n133\tA set-aware structural similarity metric.\n134\tFor a pair of entity sets $(E_a, E_b)$:\n135\t(i)~group entities by type;\n136\t(ii)~within each type $t$, compute the optimal bipartite assignment via Hungarian matching using span-overlap similarity;\n137\t(iii)~normalize by $\\max(|E_a^t|, |E_b^t|)$ to obtain $\\textsc{sj}_t$;\n138\t(iv)~average across types.\n139\tThe instance-level score is $\\textsc{sj}(x) = \\binom{N}{2}^{-1} \\sum_{i<j} \\textsc{sj}(y_i, y_j)$.\n140\tSJ generalizes to arbitrary set-structured outputs (relations, events) without task-specific design.\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Entity Voting Confidence (\\textsc{vote}).}\n143\tFor each entity $e$ in the greedy output $y_0$, compute $v(e) = |\\{i : e \\in y_i\\}|/N$ (the fraction of samples containing $e$).\n144\tThe instance-level score is $\\textsc{vote}(x) = |y_0|^{-1} \\sum_{e \\in y_0} v(e)$.\n145\tUnlike SJ, voting is entity-specific and does not generalize to arbitrary structures.\n146\t\n147\t\\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\n148\t\n149\tWe evaluate quality estimation via Spearman $\\rho$, Kendall $\\tau$, and AUROC (binarized at median $F_1$) between signal scores and instance-level $F_1$.\n150\tWe evaluate sample selection via best-of-$N$ $F_1$: $F_1(\\arg\\max_{y_i} s(y_i), y^*)$, macro-averaged across instances.\n151\tWe report results on the \\emph{full} set (all instances) and the \\emph{conditional} set (instances where $F_1(y_0, y^*) > 0$), which separates model capability errors from uncertainty estimation.\n152\t\n153\t% ============================================================\n154\t% 4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n155\t% ============================================================\n156\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n157\t\\label{sec:setup}\n158\t\n159\t\\paragraph{Datasets.}\n160\t\\textbf{SciERC}~\\cite{luan2018multi}: scientific abstracts with 6 entity types and 7 relation types; we use the standard test set ($n{=}529$ for NER, $n{=}397$ for RE).\n161\t\\textbf{CoNLL-2003}~\\cite{conll2003}: news text with 4 entity types (PER, ORG, LOC, MISC); test set $n{=}3{,}453$ ($n{=}2{,}756$ valid after filtering).\n162\t\\textbf{WNUT-2017}~\\cite{derczynski2017wnut}: social media text with 6 entity types; used only for out-of-domain evaluation ($n{=}1{,}287$).\n163\t\n164\t\\paragraph{Models.}\n165\t\\textbf{Qwen3-8B}~\\cite{qwen2025}: fine-tuned with LoRA on SciERC and CoNLL-2003 training sets.\n166\t\\textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct}~\\cite{llama2024}: fine-tuned with LoRA on SciERC and CoNLL-2003.\n167\tBoth models are 8B-parameter instruction-tuned models representing distinct architecture families.\n168\t\n169\t\\paragraph{Generation.}\n170\t$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ with greedy ($T{=}0$) reference, seed 42.\n171\tFor robustness analysis, we also evaluate $N{=}16$ with three seeds (42, 123, 456) on LLaMA $\\times$ CoNLL.\n172\t\n173\t% ============================================================\n174\t% 5. RESULTS\n175\t% ============================================================\n176\t\\section{Results}\n177\t\n178\t% ---- 5.1 Quality Estimation ----\n179\t\\subsection{Quality Estimation}\n180\t\\label{sec:results-qe}\n181\t\n182\t\\begin{table}[t]\n183\t\\centering\n184\t\\caption{Quality estimation on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Spearman $\\rho$, Kendall $\\tau$, and AUROC between each signal and instance-level $F_1$. Best in \\textbf{bold}, second \\underline{underlined}.}\n185\t\\label{tab:qe-scierc}\n186\t\\small\n187\t\\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}\n188\t\\toprule\n189\t& \\multicolumn{3}{c}{\\textbf{NER} ($n{=}529$)} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{RE} ($n{=}397$)} \\\\\n190\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \\cmidrule(lr){5-6}\n191\tSignal & $\\rho$ & $\\tau$ & AUROC & $\\rho$ & AUROC \\\\\n192\t\\midrule\n193\t\\textsc{vote}   & \\textbf{0.502} & \\textbf{0.377} & \\textbf{0.728} & \\textbf{0.422} & \\textbf{0.716} \\\\\n194\t\\textsc{sj}     & \\underline{0.357} & \\underline{0.266} & \\underline{0.666} & 0.211 & 0.582 \\\\\n195\t\\textsc{fk}     & 0.265 & 0.203 & 0.624 & \\underline{0.253} & \\underline{0.631} \\\\\n196\t\\textsc{em}     & 0.257 & 0.192 & 0.623 & 0.100 & 0.504 \\\\\n197\t\\textsc{logprob}& 0.140 & 0.101 & 0.559 & 0.247 & 0.635 \\\\\n198\t\\bottomrule\n199\t\\end{tabular}\n200\t\\end{table}\n201\t\n202\t\\Cref{tab:qe-scierc} presents quality estimation results on SciERC.\n203\tOn NER, entity voting confidence achieves the strongest correlation ($\\rho{=}0.502$, AUROC${=}0.728$), followed by Soft Jaccard ($\\rho{=}0.357$, AUROC${=}0.666$).\n204\tBoth structural consistency signals substantially outperform log-probability ($\\rho{=}0.140$, AUROC${=}0.559$), with voting yielding a $+0.362$ $\\rho$ advantage and $+16.9$ AUROC points.\n205\t\n206\tOn RE, voting again leads ($\\rho{=}0.422$, AUROC${=}0.716$), but the margins narrow: FK ($\\rho{=}0.253$) and logprob ($\\rho{=}0.247$, AUROC${=}0.635$) become competitive on specific metrics.\n207\tEM is weakest on RE ($\\rho{=}0.100$), confirming that exact-match agreement is too strict for structured outputs with compositional variation.\n208\t\n209\t\\paragraph{Conditional analysis.}\n210\tFiltering to instances where $F_1(y_0) > 0$ (NER $n{=}501$; RE $n{=}296$) improves all signals.\n211\tOn NER, SJ rises from $\\rho{=}0.357$ to $0.412$ while voting moves from $0.502$ to $0.450$---SJ's relative position improves in the conditional regime.\n212\tOn RE, the conditional lift is dramatic: SJ rises from $0.211$ to $0.368$ ($+0.157$), voting from $0.422$ to $0.463$, while logprob improves only modestly ($0.247 \\to 0.146$---in fact decreasing, as logprob conflates capability gaps with uncertainty).\n213\tThis motivates the task-complexity analysis in \\cref{sec:results-boundary}.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\begin{tikzpicture}\n218\t\\begin{axis}[\n219\t    width=\\linewidth,\n220\t    height=6cm,\n221\t    ybar=2pt,\n222\t    bar width=8pt,\n223\t    ylabel={Spearman $\\rho$},\n224\t    symbolic x coords={Vote,SJ,FK,EM,Logprob},\n225\t    xtick=data,\n226\t    ymin=0, ymax=0.6,\n227\t    legend style={at={(0.98,0.98)},anchor=north east,font=\\small},\n228\t    enlarge x limits=0.15,\n229\t    nodes near coords,\n230\t    every node near coord/.append style={font=\\tiny,rotate=90,anchor=west},\n231\t    grid=major,\n232\t    grid style={dashed,gray!30},\n233\t]\n234\t\\addplot[fill=structblue!80] coordinates {(Vote,0.502) (SJ,0.357) (FK,0.265) (EM,0.257) (Logprob,0.140)};\n235\t\\addplot[fill=logprobred!60] coordinates {(Vote,0.422) (SJ,0.211) (FK,0.253) (EM,0.100) (Logprob,0.247)};\n236\t\\legend{NER,RE}\n237\t\\end{axis}\n238\t\\end{tikzpicture}\n239\t\\caption{Spearman $\\rho$ between confidence signals and instance-level $F_1$ on SciERC (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$). Structural signals (Vote, SJ) dominate on NER; on RE, logprob becomes competitive due to compound error degradation of consistency signals.}\n240\t\\label{fig:rho-comparison}\n241\t\\end{figure}\n242\t\n243\t% ---- 5.2 Generalization ----\n244\t\\subsection{Cross-Model and Cross-Dataset Generalization}\n245\t\\label{sec:results-generalization}\n246\t\n247\t\\begin{table}[t]\n248\t\\centering\n249\t\\caption{$2{\\times}2$ cross-model $\\times$ cross-dataset quality estimation (Spearman $\\rho$, full set, $N{=}8$). Structural signals outperform logprob in all four configurations. Cross-model $|\\Delta\\rho| \\le 0.006$ on the same dataset.}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LJ37fBMNoZL1Xtr8mQcjgu","session_id":"a188aefa-23b6-4688-b448-164a4ee4e586","uuid":"19dccbee-d8e5-4851-85c5-0e6a0c1d5d5f","timestamp":"2026-05-14T21:32:04.179Z"}
